  "structure": {
    "block_count": 60,
    "blocks": [
      {
        "block_id": "block_000000",
        "block_index": 0,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h1",
        "text": "Why Leaving Your Child in the Car Is So Dangerous",
        "text_sha256": "10bc5de496f8dd82caa7598a3e1a7351e84a6d717827e4d4ec561eaae01503c8",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000001",
        "block_index": 1,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "the dangers of leaving your child in a hot car, toddler boy playing with a toy car",
        "text_sha256": "6695250700edec81bbca1c67ea471a721919c668f4b8e7b5b7bbc3ee28a72f8a",
        "word_count": 17
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000002",
        "block_index": 2,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Whether it’s an accident or done on purpose while you dash into a store, leaving a baby in a parked car is never safe. Here’s more about this grave danger and how to protect your little one.",
        "text_sha256": "370277f855a393b47d53a1c7c8bcd4a9dcc0044769bb5963d6a893719bf2d2f4",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000003",
        "block_index": 3,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If you’re tempted to leave your baby in the car while you do a fast errand, you’re not alone. According to one survey, 14 percent of parents have done this exact thing. Trusted SourceSafe Kids WorldwideNew Study: 14% of Parents Say They Have Left a Child Alone Inside Parked Vehicle Despite the Risks of HeatstrokeSee All Sources [1] But the act could be deadly, as car temperatures can soar to dangerously high levels in just a few minutes.",
        "text_sha256": "e593115c21fe15c0abc3595fc9d1d6f8d47a5793af8ac5f7f23479fb119cdff2",
        "word_count": 78
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000004",
        "block_index": 4,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Trusted SourceSafe Kids WorldwideNew Study: 14% of Parents Say They Have Left a Child Alone Inside Parked Vehicle Despite the Risks of HeatstrokeSee All Sources",
        "text_sha256": "62a88c11ba83e832d85f3228a58f57d458d4a9ff15212ed307e71fc991423111",
        "word_count": 25
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000005",
        "block_index": 5,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Read on for more about how this tragedy can occur, what happens when children are left inside hot cars and some tips you can use to prevent this nightmare from ever happening to you.",
        "text_sha256": "eb507e89d04a0bdb28cc7e42950eda2047deef0585fbdfe510d45becdf11f919",
        "word_count": 34
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000006",
        "block_index": 6,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Why do parents leave kids in the car?",
        "text_sha256": "4238f59bcea834416e13ee28fceb94d075444271ef405b029c6fe233cf6e88ec",
        "word_count": 8
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000007",
        "block_index": 7,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Leaving your child unattended in the car — just for a short while — may seem innocent enough. Picture this scenario: You’ve been cooped up all day with a cranky baby and you really need to hit the store for some groceries for dinner. You buckle up your little one for the short trip, but when you get there, she’s sound asleep. It’ll take no time to grab the things you need, so why not leave her sleeping in the car seat?",
        "text_sha256": "ffa0256c44c22d3e78d9bed755cd5fec1c59809f5c84d90786ca5d817333e561",
        "word_count": 82
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000008",
        "block_index": 8,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "In a perfect world, the car doesn’t heat up enough to harm the baby, and the story ends with the parent’s swift return. But this outcome is in no way guaranteed. Leaving children in a car for any length of time is very risky and should never be attempted.",
        "text_sha256": "c25a67e141517c118577258da9e72d19c1aa27e59a167a855c466d029d7b4446",
        "word_count": 49
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000009",
        "block_index": 9,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Sometimes, however, parents leave a baby or toddler in a parked car by accident. In fact, an over two-decade review of pediatric car deaths found that the majority of fatal heatstroke cases (53 percent) happened when a caregiver had forgotten the child. Trusted SourceNational Highway Traffic Safety AdministrationYou Can Help Prevent Hot Car DeathsSee All Sources [2] Factors that can contribute to this forgetfulness include a change in routine, fatigue, stress and other distractions.",
        "text_sha256": "b944ab0622873779c7e7e35f1e4c00bc8427c27c77dec3550f86064edecb4650",
        "word_count": 74
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000010",
        "block_index": 10,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Trusted SourceNational Highway Traffic Safety AdministrationYou Can Help Prevent Hot Car DeathsSee All Sources",
        "text_sha256": "3b3ee1b8d17c345464de1382d647f76026fd23d4eeaf5016e50edd0f58de9761",
        "word_count": 14
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000011",
        "block_index": 11,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Accidentally leaving a child in a car can sometimes be chalked up to your brain not being focused on the baby being there with you. This can happen because she's in the back in a rear-facing car seat and maybe even fell asleep so she's not making any noise.",
        "text_sha256": "0ef6dce6f9461e1ab2efe1eaa8f1c16e1e9bac54602e616c055848e0bef64f53",
        "word_count": 49
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000012",
        "block_index": 12,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "This can also occur when a parent follows her normal routine as if on auto-pilot, not realizing that something is different. (For example, it was her turn to drop the baby off at day care.) According to specialists, this is more likely to occur when a parent is very tired or under stress.",
        "text_sha256": "e47862bb8e4f63519b7e216b30d05e97e77086dcfe751e203c44762bc9f11c3a",
        "word_count": 53
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000013",
        "block_index": 13,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "What happens when a child is left in a hot car?",
        "text_sha256": "474626702e8526a3a5e2ed9eed5c2b118fba7cfd8dbaddc30fd82647a44026b5",
        "word_count": 11
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000014",
        "block_index": 14,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Every year dozens of children, most under the age of 3, die after being left — usually sleeping — inside the car seat of a vehicle. When left alone in a car, kids can contract heatstroke, which is defined as a core body temperature of greater than or equal to 104 degrees Fahrenheit accompanied by central nervous system dysfunction. Heatstroke is among the leading causes of non-crash, vehicle-related deaths of children, according to the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA). Trusted SourceNational Highway Traffic Safety AdministrationChild SafetySee All Sources [3]",
        "text_sha256": "79dd2902806df10e7476d49bceebf6d4a9516d2660f1bf562fc7ea7c6056968a",
        "word_count": 90
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000015",
        "block_index": 15,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Trusted SourceNational Highway Traffic Safety AdministrationChild SafetySee All Sources",
        "text_sha256": "ee7035b405200e16f7ddd9fa71e583cb4f665be981b5017faaa93b6dc731793b",
        "word_count": 9
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000016",
        "block_index": 16,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "While these car deaths typically happen in the summer and hot climates, NHTSA notes that your car can surpass 110 degrees F even when outside temperatures are as low as the 60s. A car becomes like an oven, rising nearly 20 degrees F in 10 minutes. In fact, one study found that car interiors can reach 117 degrees F when it’s only 72 degrees F outside. A baby can die when her body temperature reaches 107 degrees F.",
        "text_sha256": "14a6f0dc43664a436f7e6c3ea72f5068488727d637c6824e8e74e0e6481595b4",
        "word_count": 78
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000017",
        "block_index": 17,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "And if you think opening the car window can help, you’re mistaken. Cracking the car windows doesn't keep the vehicle’s interior temperature from rising or lower the maximum temperature. Even with the windows left ajar, the temperature inside a car can rise to 125 degrees F in just minutes.",
        "text_sha256": "9bac0efdbfbb6c0a47f0396b8708925e21553c08f9ab95d3295e9e8079967243",
        "word_count": 49
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000018",
        "block_index": 18,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "What’s more, kids are especially vulnerable to hot conditions as their bodies absorb more heat quicker than adults’ bodies, and their temperatures rise up to five times faster. And since babies don’t sweat as much as grown-ups, they can’t cool off as easily, leaving them more susceptible to high heat.",
        "text_sha256": "134ccf7c5b782ce818827a4c70a3bcd0fe11e146a682daf131b912037bd682d6",
        "word_count": 50
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000019",
        "block_index": 19,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Since 1990, 54 percent of heatstroke deaths in cars occurred in children 1 year old or younger. Trusted SourceKidsandCars.orgChild Hot Car Dangers Fact SheetSee All Sources [4] This may be because it’s hard to tell from the driver’s vantage point whether a rear-facing car seat is occupied or empty.",
        "text_sha256": "90dcfff0cded7e738182735dd6344c08805c4ddf954c49805b7e9df11e42311e",
        "word_count": 49
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000020",
        "block_index": 20,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Trusted SourceKidsandCars.orgChild Hot Car Dangers Fact SheetSee All Sources",
        "text_sha256": "80de07e59ec62ed145531c3d42ee76872c4cf3bfecda451d4984e702de5d5b54",
        "word_count": 9
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000021",
        "block_index": 21,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Is it illegal to leave your child in the car?",
        "text_sha256": "2e3257526c6709dd50e555fe467bfca38e6d2719e695f6282ab703567b700c32",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000022",
        "block_index": 22,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Yes, in some parts of the country it’s illegal to leave your child alone in a parked car, while in other areas, proposed laws against the practice are in the works. Although the laws vary state by state, here are the 20 states where it’s currently unlawful to leave your children in a car unattended: Trusted SourceKidsandCars.orgState LawsSee All Sources [5]",
        "text_sha256": "edd8d365808d2aa00776d700da4c693d1c561a039cf0fcdb2e56d438ab043674",
        "word_count": 61
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000023",
